* op-point-sanity -- DC operating-point sanity testbench for ldo_core (issue #8)
*
* Confirms the core's feedback loop closes sensibly with the placeholder
* error amp: Vout near the 1.8 V target, no rail-stuck nodes, sane supply
* current. This is a loop-closure sanity check, NOT a spec-line
* measurement -- see sim/README.md and sim/op-point-sanity/records/ for the
* recorded result and the evidence-record format, and design/README.md for
* the ldo_core interface this testbench instantiates.
*
* ERRAMP_OUT and PASS_GATE are ldo_core's deliberately-separate loop-break
* ports (the node a future stability testbench, #10, breaks for a
* Middlebrook/Tian injection). For NORMAL operation, this testbench just
* ties them to the same node (LOOP) at instantiation -- a plain wire, done
* here at the testbench level rather than inside the core schematic, so
* the core needs no rewiring either way.
*
* Template: run.sh substitutes the @TOKENS@ below (PDK paths, corner
* sections, temperature, supply, enable state) and runs the result with
* `ngspice -b`.

.include "@DESIGN_INCLUDE@"
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @MOS_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @RES_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @BJT_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @DIODE_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @MOSCAP_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @MIMCAP_CORNER@

.temp @TEMP_C@

.include "@LDO_NETLIST@"

* --- stimulus ----------------------------------------------------------
Vin VIN 0 DC @VIN_V@
Ven EN 0 DC @EN_V@

* --- DUT -----------------------------------------------------------------
* Port order (design/ldo_core.sym / .sch): VIN VOUT EN VSS ERRAMP_OUT PASS_GATE VREF
* --- Reference (issue #174 / DR-0021) ------------------------------------
* VREF is a TOP-LEVEL PORT of ldo_core as of #174; it used to be an internal
* net driven by Vref1, an ideal 1.2 V vsource instantiated INSIDE the
* subcircuit. That source is deleted from design/ldo_core.sch and reappears
* here, unchanged, at deck level. This is an electrical no-op by
* construction: an ideal vsource is an ideal vsource wherever it is
* declared, its return was already the cell's VSS port (node 0 here), and it
* is an AC short to ground in both placements -- so DC operating points, .ac
* transfers and transients are all identical to the pre-#174 deck. The point
* of the move is that the idealization is now visible at the interface and a
* real reference (a bandgap block, or the Chipalooza Challenge #5 harness's
* bandgap-referenced bias-voltage slot) can drive it without editing the
* schematic. One source serves every DUT instance in this deck: an ideal
* source has zero output impedance, so instances cannot interact through it.
Vref VREF 0 DC 1.2

Xdut VIN VOUT EN 0 LOOP LOOP VREF ldo_core

* --- output network (testbench-side, per issue #8 guidance) --------------
* A light, realistic load (1 mA) plus the compensation cap the ratified
* spec calls external-cap, output-pole-dominant (0.33-4.7 uF effective
* range; 1 uF + 100 mOhm ESR chosen here, in range).
Rload VOUT 0 1.8k
Resr VOUT n_esr 0.1
Cout n_esr 0 1u

.control
op
let m_vout = v(vout)
let m_vin = v(vin)
let m_loop = v(loop)
let m_fb = v(xdut.fb)
* VREF is now a direct top-level port wired 1:1 to this deck's own VREF
* net (issue #174 / DR-0021) -- ngspice folds a straight-through port like
* that into the top-level node during flattening rather than keeping a
* distinct xdut.vref vector, so v(xdut.vref) is no longer resolvable (it
* was, when VREF was genuinely internal, driven by Vref1). v(vref) reads
* the same physical node.
let m_vref = v(vref)
let m_ivin_ma = -i(vin)*1e3
let m_ien_ua = -i(ven)*1e6
print m_vout
print m_vin
print m_loop
print m_fb
print m_vref
print m_ivin_ma
print m_ien_ua
.endc
.end
